{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>He made him ride on the high places of the earth, etc.</b> In the word ״בָּמֳתֵי״ there is a superfluous ו״ו, and Ba'al ha-Turim explained it as he explained. But I say that this superfluous ו״ו is an allusion to the six times that אֶרֶץ is mentioned in parashat Ekev (Deuteronomy 8:7–9) in praise of the land of Israel. Therefore it says ״בָּמֳתֵי אָרֶץ״, as if it said six אֶרֶץ, and it is easy to understand. After that it says: ״וַיֵּנִקֵהוּ דְבַשׁ מִסֶּלַע״ — in the word ״וַיֵּנִקֵהוּ״ the יוֹ״ד is missing. It appears to me that he alluded by this to the fact that because He nursed them with honey from the rock, Jeshurun grew fat and kicked, and they forgot the Torah that is included in the Ten Commandments. And this is more correct than what Ba'al ha-Turim explained. Likewise ״צוּר יְלָדְךָ תֶּשִׁי״ — the יוֹ״ד is small, because by transgressing the Ten Commandments they caused a weakening of strength on high, as it were. And concerning the word ״יַצֵּב גְּבֻלֹת עַמִּים״, Ba'al ha-Turim explained: ״גְּבֻלֹת״ is written deficient in ו״ו, to say twelve mazzalot corresponding to the number of the children of Israel — interpretation according to his interpretation, for the ו״ו that is hidden from it is also a ו״ו, and thus the mazzalot are six ministers by day, the revealed, and six ministers by night, the hidden. And since there is no mazzal for Israel (Shabbat 156a), therefore ״גְּבֻלֹת״ is deficient in ו״ו, to say that they [the mazzalot] have no dominion over the twelve tribes but only over the nations. Therefore it says: ״יַצֵּב גְּבֻלֹת עַמִּים״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:00.249684777Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}